    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old man presents with a solitary, destructive, radiolucent mass in the mandible. Biopsy reveals sheets of monoclonal plasma cells. A complete skeletal survey and bone marrow biopsy are negative for other lesions. What is the diagnosis?', '["Multiple myeloma", "Solitary extramedullary plasmacytoma", "Solitary plasmacytoma of bone", "Osteosarcoma", "Ameloblastoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A solitary plasmacytoma of bone consists of a single discrete mass of monoclonal plasma cells in the bone without evidence of systemic multiple myeloma (no hypercalcemia, renal failure, or anemia). The mandible and maxilla are common sites in the head and neck.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient diagnosed with a solitary extramedullary plasmacytoma of the sinonasal tract is treated with definitive radiation therapy. What is the most critical aspect of this patient''s long-term follow-up?', '["Monitoring for loss of smell", "Monitoring for malignant transformation into squamous cell carcinoma", "Surveillance for progression to systemic Multiple Myeloma", "Monitoring for radiation-induced cataracts", "Monthly sinus endoscopies"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A significant percentage (up to 30-50% over years) of patients with solitary plasmacytomas (both intraosseous and extramedullary) will eventually progress to systemic Multiple Myeloma, requiring vigilant long-term surveillance with serum/urine protein electrophoresis and skeletal surveys.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old woman presents with a slow-growing, painless, firm mass on the dorsal surface of her tongue. Biopsy reveals a benign tumor composed of large, polygonal cells with abundant granular, eosinophilic cytoplasm. Immunohistochemistry is strongly positive for S-100 protein. What is the diagnosis?', '["Squamous cell carcinoma", "Granular cell tumor", "Schwannoma", "Hemangioma", "Lipoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A granular cell tumor is a benign neoplasm thought to be of Schwann cell origin (hence the S-100 positivity). The oral cavity, specifically the dorsum of the tongue, is the most common site of presentation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What histopathologic finding in the epithelium overlying a granular cell tumor can frequently lead to a misdiagnosis of squamous cell carcinoma if the biopsy is too superficial?', '["Severe dysplasia", "Pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia", "Keratin pearls", "Viral cytopathic effect", "Melanocytic atypia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The squamous epithelium overlying a granular cell tumor often exhibits pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia (benign, reactive downward growth of epithelium). A shallow biopsy that misses the underlying granular cells can easily be misread as invasive SCC.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 5-year-old boy presents with a rapidly enlarging, painless mass in his right orbit causing proptosis, along with a firm mass in the right neck. Biopsy of the orbital mass reveals a "small round blue cell" tumor with strap cells and cross-striations. Immunohistochemistry is positive for desmin and myogenin. What is the diagnosis?', '["Neuroblastoma", "Retinoblastoma", "Rhabdomyosarcoma", "Lymphoma", "Ewing sarcoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Rhabdomyosarcoma is the most common soft tissue sarcoma in children. The head and neck (particularly the orbit and parameningeal sites) is the most common location. Positive staining for muscle markers (desmin, myogenin, MyoD1) is diagnostic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which histologic subtype of rhabdomyosarcoma is the most common in the pediatric head and neck and carries the most favorable prognosis?', '["Alveolar", "Pleomorphic", "Embryonal", "Spindle cell", "Botryoid"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma is the most common subtype in children and generally has a better prognosis than the alveolar subtype (which is more common in extremities and has a specific t(2;13) translocation).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoes a radical neck dissection. Postoperatively, she develops ptosis (drooping eyelid), miosis (constricted pupil), and anhidrosis (lack of sweating) on the ipsilateral side of her face. Which nervous system structure was inadvertently injured?', '["The facial nerve", "The oculomotor nerve", "The cervical sympathetic chain", "The vagus nerve", "The trigeminal nerve"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This triad describes Horner''s syndrome. The cervical sympathetic trunk lies deep to the carotid sheath on the prevertebral fascia. Injury disrupts sympathetic innervation to the eye and face.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a parotidectomy for a malignant tumor, the surgeon sacrifices the facial nerve to achieve clear margins. To immediately restore some resting facial tone and protect the eye, the surgeon decides to perform a static sling procedure. What autologous material is most commonly harvested to create this sling?', '["Fascia lata (from the thigh)", "A segment of the saphenous vein", "A rib cartilage graft", "A strip of the platysma muscle", "A piece of the temporal bone"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Fascia lata is the preferred autologous material for static facial suspension because it is strong, abundant, and does not stretch significantly over time, providing excellent mechanical support for the drooping corner of the mouth and cheek.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'For dynamic facial reanimation following permanent facial nerve sacrifice, a nerve graft can be connected from the functioning contralateral facial nerve to the paralyzed ipsilateral facial muscles. What is this specific nerve grafting technique called?', '["Hypoglossal-facial nerve anastomosis", "Cross-facial nerve graft", "Masseteric nerve transfer", "Spinal accessory-facial nerve transfer", "Phrenic nerve transfer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A cross-facial nerve graft (usually utilizing the sural nerve as an extended cable) borrows redundant branches from the healthy side and routes them across the face to innervate the paralyzed side, uniquely offering the possibility of spontaneous, emotion-driven smiling.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old male with a history of radiation therapy for tonsil cancer presents with trismus (inability to open his mouth fully). Examination reveals no evidence of recurrent tumor. What is the most likely cause of his trismus?', '["Dislocation of the temporomandibular joint", "Radiation-induced fibrosis of the masticatory muscles (e.g., medial pterygoid and masseter)", "Osteomyelitis of the maxilla", "Damage to the trigeminal nerve", "Tetanus infection"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Radiation to the oropharynx frequently includes the pterygoid muscles in the radiation field. Late radiation toxicity causes severe, progressive fibrosis of these muscles, leading to profound and difficult-to-treat trismus.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following interventions is the most effective preventative measure for radiation-induced trismus in head and neck cancer patients?', '["Prophylactic botulinum toxin injections", "Aggressive, daily jaw-stretching exercises (e.g., using a TheraBite device) initiated before and maintained during and after radiation therapy", "Systemic muscle relaxants", "Surgical release of the masseter muscle prior to radiation", "Hyperbaric oxygen therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Once radiation fibrosis sets in, it is nearly irreversible. Prevention through rigorous, consistent, daily physical therapy (jaw stretching) is the only highly effective method to maintain mouth opening.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with an advanced laryngeal cancer is found to have direct invasion of the tumor into the prevertebral fascia. How does this finding affect the surgical management?', '["It requires a concurrent neurosurgical spine fusion", "It mandates a prophylactic tracheostomy", "It renders the tumor anatomically unresectable, as the prevertebral fascia is a boundary that cannot be safely resected en bloc with the larynx", "It requires the removal of the anterior arch of the cervical vertebrae", "It has no impact on surgical resectability"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Fixation of a tumor to the prevertebral fascia (or invasion of the carotid artery) generally classifies a head and neck squamous cell carcinoma as unresectable (T4b), shifting the treatment intent from primary surgery to definitive chemoradiation or palliation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the classic presenting symptom of a glomus vagale (a paraganglioma of the vagus nerve)?', '["Pulsatile tinnitus", "A slow-growing, painless neck mass located high in the neck behind the angle of the mandible, frequently causing an ipsilateral vocal cord paralysis (hoarseness)", "Severe dysphagia", "Facial nerve paralysis", "Anosmia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Unlike carotid body tumors which present lower at the bifurcation, glomus vagale tumors arise higher near the skull base. Because they originate within the vagus nerve, CN X palsy (vocal cord paralysis) is a very common and early presenting sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A newborn presents with a massive, firm, neck mass causing severe airway obstruction and dysphagia. Imaging shows a heterogeneous mass with solid, cystic, and heavily calcified components. Biopsy reveals tissues derived from all three embryonic germ layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, endoderm). What is the diagnosis?', '["Cystic hygroma", "Cervical teratoma", "Thyroglossal duct cyst", "Neuroblastoma", "Rhabdomyosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A cervical teratoma is a rare congenital tumor containing tissues from all three germ layers (e.g., hair, cartilage, respiratory epithelium). They are often massive at birth, requiring immediate airway securement (EXIT procedure).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary risk associated with a cervical teratoma if it is not completely surgically excised?', '["It will spontaneously regress", "It will cause permanent deafness", "There is a risk of malignant transformation (most commonly into a squamous cell carcinoma or sarcoma) later in life, particularly in adults", "It will cause severe hyperthyroidism", "It will spread contagiously"]'::jsonb, 2, 'While most congenital cervical teratomas are benign, incomplete excision leaves tissue that has a significant risk of undergoing malignant transformation as the patient ages.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old male with a 40-pack-year smoking history presents with a 2-cm ulcerated lesion on the floor of the mouth. Biopsy confirms squamous cell carcinoma. He also complains of recent onset of left-sided chest pain and a chronic cough. A chest X-ray shows a 3-cm spiculated mass in the left upper lobe of the lung. What is the most appropriate oncologic term for the lung mass?', '["A distant metastasis from the floor of mouth cancer", "A synchronous primary tumor", "A metachronous primary tumor", "A benign granuloma", "A lymphatic spread"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Patients with head and neck SCC have a high rate (up to 10-20%) of developing a second primary tumor, most commonly in the lungs or esophagus, due to "field cancerization" from tobacco/alcohol. Because it was found at the same time (or within 6 months), it is a synchronous primary, not a metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What concept explains why a patient who has been cured of one tobacco-related oral cavity cancer remains at a very high lifelong risk of developing a second, distinct primary cancer in the pharynx, larynx, or lungs?', '["Genetic anticipation", "Field cancerization (the entire mucosal surface exposed to the carcinogen has accumulated genetic mutations, making it a fertile ''field'' for independent tumors to arise)", "Metastatic seeding during the first surgery", "Radiation-induced mutagenesis", "Immunosuppression from chemotherapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Field cancerization is the principle that carcinogens bathe the entire upper aerodigestive tract, causing widespread early genetic alterations. Even if one tumor is removed, the remaining mucosa is highly primed to form new, independent cancers.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient receives definitive radiation therapy for an early-stage glottic cancer. Ten years later, a new squamous cell carcinoma develops on the previously irradiated true vocal cord. This is considered a recurrence. What is the standard, most reliable curative treatment option for this radiation-failure recurrence?', '["A second full course of radiation therapy", "Systemic chemotherapy", "Surgical resection (salvage total or partial laryngectomy)", "Immunotherapy alone", "Photodynamic therapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Once a tissue field has received definitive (curative-dose) radiation, it generally cannot be re-irradiated with a curative intent without causing catastrophic tissue necrosis. Surgical salvage is the only reliable curative option for radiation failures.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old female presents with a slowly enlarging, painless mass in the deep lobe of the parotid gland. Imaging suggests a pleomorphic adenoma. During surgical excision, the tumor is accidentally ruptured, and tumor contents spill into the surgical field. What is the most significant consequence of this intraoperative event?', '["Immediate anaphylaxis", "Malignant transformation of the spilled cells", "A drastically increased risk of multifocal tumor recurrence in the surgical bed years later", "Severe postoperative infection", "Permanent facial nerve paralysis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pleomorphic adenomas have a high propensity for seeding. If the tumor capsule is breached and contents spill into the wound, the patient is at very high risk for developing multiple, highly difficult-to-treat recurrent nodules ("recurrent pleomorphic adenoma").'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with an advanced cancer of the base of the tongue undergoes a total glossectomy (removal of the entire tongue). What is the most profound and immediate functional deficit this patient will face, requiring aggressive rehabilitation?', '["Loss of taste", "Inability to breathe through the nose", "Severe, intractable aspiration (inability to safely swallow without food/liquid entering the airway) and complete loss of articulate speech", "Loss of the gag reflex", "Inability to open the mouth"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The tongue is the primary organ of oral transit and articulation. Without it, patients cannot propel a food bolus backward safely, leading to profound aspiration. They also lose the ability to form most consonants, severely impairing speech.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'To mitigate the severe aspiration risk after a total glossectomy, what reconstructive strategy is typically employed?', '["Using a thin skin graft to line the floor of the mouth", "Reconstructing a massive, bulky, non-mobile \"dummy\" tongue using a large free flap (e.g., ALT or rectus abdominis) to fill the oral cavity and guide food laterally into the pharynx", "Sewing the lips shut", "Leaving the defect open to heal by secondary intention", "Using a titanium plate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A massive, bulky flap is placed to occupy the space of the missing tongue. While it cannot move to articulate speech or actively push food, its sheer bulk prevents food from pooling and helps gravity direct the bolus past the airway into the esophagus.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 40-year-old male presents with a persistent, painless, hard, 1.5 cm lymph node in the left submandibular triangle (Level Ib). He uses smokeless tobacco. Examination of the oral cavity is negative. What easily overlooked site must be meticulously examined and palpated in this specific clinical scenario?', '["The nasopharynx", "The vocal cords", "The floor of the mouth and the ventral surface of the tongue", "The thyroid gland", "The paranasal sinuses"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Level Ib nodes primarily drain the oral cavity (floor of mouth, anterior tongue, lower gingiva). A hard node here in a tobacco user strongly points to an occult oral cavity primary, which can easily hide in the loose mucosa of the floor of the mouth.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient presents with a rapidly growing, highly vascular, friable tumor on the anterior nasal septum. They report frequent, severe nosebleeds. Biopsy reveals a lobular proliferation of capillaries. The patient is a 28-year-old pregnant woman. What is the diagnosis?', '["Squamous cell carcinoma", "Esthesioneuroblastoma", "Pyogenic granuloma (lobular capillary hemangioma)", "Inverted papilloma", "Hemangiopericytoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pyogenic granulomas frequently occur on the nasal septum (and oral gingiva) in pregnant women due to hormonal influences. They are benign but bleed profusely.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old man presents with a hard, painless mass in the right cheek. Imaging shows a tumor arising from the buccal mucosa and invading the underlying masseter muscle and the mandible. What is the clinical T stage (AJCC 8th edition) of this oral cavity squamous cell carcinoma?', '["T1", "T2", "T3", "T4a (moderately advanced local disease)", "T4b (very advanced local disease)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In the oral cavity, invasion through cortical bone (mandible), into the deep/extrinsic muscles of the tongue, or into the maxillary sinus/skin of face classifies the tumor as T4a. (T4b involves the masticator space, pterygoid plates, or skull base).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following describes the mechanism of action of Pembrolizumab and Nivolumab, which are increasingly used in recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma?', '["They directly inhibit the EGFR tyrosine kinase", "They bind to VEGF to prevent tumor angiogenesis", "They are monoclonal antibodies that block the PD-1 receptor on T-cells, reversing immune exhaustion and allowing the immune system to attack the tumor", "They intercalate into DNA to prevent replication", "They destroy the tumor''s blood supply"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pembrolizumab and Nivolumab are immune checkpoint inhibitors. By blocking the PD-1/PD-L1 interaction, they prevent the tumor from "turning off" the body''s cytotoxic T-cells, allowing a robust anti-tumor immune response.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is a necessary prerequisite for determining if a patient with recurrent/metastatic head and neck SCC will likely benefit from Pembrolizumab therapy?', '["The tumor must be HPV-positive", "Testing the tumor biopsy for PD-L1 expression (e.g., using the Combined Positive Score, CPS)", "The tumor must have a BRAF mutation", "The patient must have failed radiation therapy", "The patient must have a high white blood cell count"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pembrolizumab''s efficacy is highly correlated with the tumor''s expression of the PD-L1 ligand. A CPS score is calculated, and patients with a CPS > 1 (and especially > 20) derive the most benefit.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with an advanced base of tongue cancer is treated with concurrent chemoradiation. One year later, a PET scan shows a hypermetabolic focus at the primary site, and a deep biopsy confirms recurrent squamous cell carcinoma. What is the only curative option remaining?', '["More radiation therapy", "Systemic chemotherapy", "Immunotherapy", "Surgical salvage (e.g., total glossectomy with flap reconstruction)", "Palliative care"]'::jsonb, 3, 'When definitive chemoradiation fails, the tissue cannot be re-irradiated. Salvage surgery, though highly morbid, is the only option that offers a chance of cure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient presents with a slowly growing, painless, firm mass in the lateral neck. Biopsy reveals a benign tumor characterized by a proliferation of uniform, spindle-shaped cells arranged around branching, "staghorn" or "antler-like" thin-walled blood vessels. What is the diagnosis?', '["Hemangioma", "Schwannoma", "Hemangiopericytoma (Solitary Fibrous Tumor)", "Fibromatosis colli", "Glomus tumor"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The histologic description of a "staghorn" vascular pattern with uniform spindle cells is the classic hallmark of a hemangiopericytoma (now classified under the umbrella of Solitary Fibrous Tumors).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary treatment for a hemangiopericytoma of the head and neck?', '["Radiation therapy", "Chemotherapy", "Complete surgical excision with negative margins", "Embolization alone", "Observation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'While generally considered benign, hemangiopericytomas can be locally aggressive and have a distinct, unpredictable potential for distant metastasis or malignant transformation. Complete surgical excision is the treatment of choice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old woman undergoes a total thyroidectomy for a 4-cm Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma. Postoperatively, her voice is normal, but she experiences severe muscle cramps, tingling in her fingers and around her mouth, and a positive Chvostek''s sign. What medication must be administered immediately?', '["Levothyroxine", "Intravenous or oral Calcium (and Vitamin D / calcitriol)", "Beta-blockers", "Corticosteroids", "Potassium"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient is exhibiting classic signs of acute hypocalcemia due to inadvertent injury, devascularization, or removal of the parathyroid glands during the thyroidectomy. Immediate calcium supplementation is required to prevent tetany and arrhythmias.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "TNM" staging system primarily based on in Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma?', '["T = Tumor Grade, N = Number of nodes, M = Mutational burden", "T = Tumor Size/Extent, N = Regional Lymph Node involvement, M = Distant Metastasis", "T = Time since diagnosis, N = Nerve involvement, M = Margins", "T = Tumor depth, N = Necrosis, M = Mitotic rate", "T = Type of cell, N = Neck level, M = Medical comorbidities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The AJCC TNM staging system relies on the size or local invasive extent of the primary Tumor (T), the presence, size, and number of regional lymph Node metastases (N), and the presence of Distant Metastasis (M).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In the 8th edition of the AJCC staging system, how does the staging for HPV-positive (p16+) oropharyngeal cancer differ drastically from HPV-negative oropharyngeal cancer?', '["It uses tumor grade instead of size", "It downstages the nodal (N) category significantly because HPV+ patients have a much better survival rate even with multiple large lymph nodes involved", "It automatically stages all HPV+ tumors as Stage IV", "It does not use the M category", "It bases staging solely on age"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because HPV+ oropharyngeal cancer is highly responsive to treatment and has excellent survival outcomes despite early nodal metastasis, the 8th edition staging was completely rewritten to downstage these patients. A patient with small primary tumor and multiple large nodes might be Stage IV if HPV-, but only Stage I or II if HPV+.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 65-year-old male presents with a rock-hard, fixed mass in the anterior neck overlying the thyroid, hoarseness, and dyspnea. He has a history of a long-standing, untreated multinodular goiter. FNA reveals highly pleomorphic, giant, and spindle cells. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Papillary thyroid carcinoma", "Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma", "Medullary thyroid carcinoma", "Thyroid lymphoma", "Riedel''s thyroiditis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma frequently arises from the dedifferentiation of a pre-existing well-differentiated thyroid cancer or a long-standing goiter. It is characterized by explosive growth, invasion of local structures (causing hoarseness/dyspnea), and highly anaplastic, bizarre cells on cytology.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most common benign neoplasm of the larynx?', '["Squamous papilloma (laryngeal papilloma)", "Vocal cord nodule", "Hemangioma", "Chondroma", "Granular cell tumor"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Squamous papillomas (caused by HPV 6 and 11) are the most common benign neoplasms of the larynx in both children and adults. Note that vocal cord nodules and polyps are inflammatory/reactive lesions, not true neoplasms.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old male with a history of intravenous drug use and hepatitis C presents with a rapidly growing, painless, violaceous nodule on his lip. Biopsy shows spindle cells forming slit-like vascular channels containing red blood cells. What is the diagnosis?', '["Kaposi sarcoma", "Squamous cell carcinoma", "Pyogenic granuloma", "Hemangioma", "Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Kaposi sarcoma (caused by HHV-8) classically presents as violaceous lesions. Histologically, it features spindle cells forming slit-like vascular spaces. While classically associated with HIV, it can occur in other immunosuppressed states or endemically.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient presents with a slowly enlarging, painless mass in the parotid gland. Surgical excision reveals a benign tumor characterized by a dense proliferation of mature lymphocytes forming germinal centers, completely replacing the normal parotid tissue, with no epithelial atypia. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Warthin tumor", "MALT lymphoma", "Benign lymphoepithelial lesion (often associated with HIV or Sjogren''s syndrome)", "Pleomorphic adenoma", "Acinic cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Benign lymphoepithelial lesions present as painless parotid swelling and are characterized by massive lymphoid infiltration. In the setting of HIV, they often present as multiple bilateral parotid cysts. In Sjogren''s, they represent the autoimmune destruction of the gland.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old female presents with severe, episodic hypertension, tachycardia, sweating, and a neck mass. Evaluation reveals a functional, catecholamine-secreting paraganglioma of the neck. What other location MUST be imaged to rule out a synchronous, and often more dangerous, tumor?', '["The brain", "The adrenal glands (to rule out a pheochromocytoma)", "The lungs", "The thyroid", "The ovaries"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Up to 10% of paragangliomas are associated with familial syndromes (e.g., MEN 2, SDH mutations). A patient with a functional neck paraganglioma has a very high risk of having a synchronous pheochromocytoma in the adrenal medulla, which must be addressed first to prevent perioperative death.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which genetic mutation is most strongly associated with familial paraganglioma syndromes, carrying a high risk of multifocal and malignant paragangliomas?', '["RET proto-oncogene", "TP53", "Succinate dehydrogenase (SDH) complex mutations (specifically SDHB and SDHD)", "PTCH1", "APC"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Mutations in the SDH complex (part of the Krebs cycle/electron transport chain) predispose patients to familial paraganglioma syndromes. SDHB mutations, in particular, carry a significantly higher risk of malignant transformation and distant metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old man presents with a hard, 2-cm mass in the left submandibular gland. He has a history of a squamous cell carcinoma of the lower lip treated 3 years ago. What is the most likely diagnosis of the submandibular mass?', '["A new primary adenoid cystic carcinoma", "Metastatic squamous cell carcinoma from the previous lip primary", "Pleomorphic adenoma", "Warthin tumor", "Sialolithiasis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The submandibular nodes (Level Ib), which lie intimately on and within the fascia of the submandibular gland, are the primary drainage basin for the lateral lower lip. A new mass here in a patient with a history of lip cancer must be presumed to be a regional metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the classic staging modality to definitively evaluate for distant metastasis in a patient with advanced (Stage IV) squamous cell carcinoma of the head and neck prior to initiating massive, morbid surgery or chemoradiation?', '["Bone scan", "CT scan of the head without contrast", "FDG-PET/CT scan from the skull base to the mid-thigh", "Ultrasound of the liver", "Chest X-ray"]'::jsonb, 2, 'FDG-PET/CT is the gold standard for detecting distant metastases (most commonly to the lungs, liver, and bones) and synchronous primary tumors, fundamentally altering the treatment plan from curative intent to palliation if distant disease is found.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is a key advantage of utilizing Transoral Robotic Surgery (TORS) for the resection of an early-stage oropharyngeal carcinoma (e.g., tonsil or base of tongue) compared to traditional open surgical approaches?', '["It guarantees a 100% cure rate", "It eliminates the need for a neck dissection", "It avoids a lip-splitting or mandible-splitting incision, significantly reducing cosmetic deformity, reducing recovery time, and potentially preserving better long-term swallowing function", "It does not require general anesthesia", "It allows the tumor to be removed without clear margins"]'::jsonb, 2, 'TORS allows the surgeon to access and resect tumors in the deep, narrow confines of the oropharynx through the open mouth using robotic arms, avoiding the extreme morbidity and scarring of a mandibulotomy (splitting the jaw).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient receives a high dose of radiation therapy to the larynx and neck. Thirty years later, the patient develops a rapidly growing, highly destructive mass within the previously irradiated field. Biopsy confirms a high-grade sarcoma. What is this phenomenon called?', '["Field cancerization", "Radiation-induced sarcoma", "Recurrent laryngeal carcinoma", "Metastatic disease", "Second primary squamous cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Radiation-induced sarcomas (e.g., osteosarcoma, fibrosarcoma, malignant fibrous histiocytoma) are a rare but devastating late complication of radiation therapy. They typically occur decades after the exposure and are notoriously aggressive and difficult to treat.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most appropriate initial management for a patient presenting with a newly discovered, asymptomatic, 2-cm, cystic midline neck mass that moves vertically with swallowing?', '["Immediate surgical excision", "Fine-needle aspiration (FNA) and an ultrasound of the neck", "Empiric antibiotics", "Incision and drainage", "Observation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mass is highly suspicious for a thyroglossal duct cyst. An ultrasound is required to confirm the cystic nature of the mass AND to ensure that a normal thyroid gland exists low in the neck (to prevent accidentally removing the patient''s only functional ectopic thyroid tissue). FNA rules out malignancy within the cyst.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "Gold Standard" treatment for an advanced, T4a squamous cell carcinoma of the larynx (tumor invading through the thyroid cartilage)?', '["Definitive radiation therapy alone", "Systemic chemotherapy alone", "Total laryngectomy with bilateral neck dissection, followed by postoperative radiation or chemoradiation", "Transoral laser microsurgery", "Observation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Once a laryngeal tumor destroys the cartilage framework (T4a), the larynx is structurally incompetent and responds poorly to radiation. Total laryngectomy is the standard of care to achieve local control and cure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a massive tumor of the anterior skull base requires a craniofacial resection. Which medical specialist is absolutely essential to co-surge with the Otolaryngologist (ENT) during this complex procedure?', '["Cardiothoracic surgeon", "Neurosurgeon", "Orthopedic surgeon", "Vascular surgeon", "General surgeon"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A craniofacial resection involves entering the anterior cranial fossa. A neurosurgeon is required to perform the craniotomy, elevate the frontal lobes, protect the brain, and assist in the en bloc resection of the cribriform plate and skull base.')
